00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.070 align:start position:0%
on her last day as international  criminal court prosecutor  fatou bensouda requested authorization  to formally  investigate philippine president rodrigo  duterte for possible crimes  against humanity it's a farewell move  that families of many of those killed in  the so-called  war on drugs are grateful for  since 2018 duterte's policy has been  subjected to preliminary examination  the international criminal court has  been analyzing a large amount of public  information in addition to the  complaints and supplementary reports  submitted by international rights groups  and lawyers here representing families  prosecutor bensouda says her examination  found that  extrajudicial killings in the  philippines have mostly been state  sanctioned  since duterte took office as president  in 2016  thousands of filipinos have been killed  the government puts the number  at around seven thousand but rights  groups believe  deaths in police-related anti-drug  operations  may reach nearly thirty thousand it  brings the message that from the top  down  whether directly or indirectly you  participate  golden induce justify encourage  cover up in whatever way you may  be liable the philippines has formally  withdrawn as a signatory to the rome  statute  and the government has repeatedly said  it will not take part in any icc  proceedings  the decision to move forward into a  formal investigation  stage is legally erroneous politically  motivated but what is clear now  is that a unilateral withdrawal will not  prevent the chamber  from continuing to look into possible  crimes  he has been belittling this institution  for so long  but what we believe it just  masks his insecurity  actually deep down i think he believes  that the iccs  as an institution has the capacity to  enforce  its decisions the government says it  regrets the actions taken  by prosecutor ben souda and that the  justice department here  continues to reinvestigate cases of  fatalities  in drug related operations but for the  families of the victims  the icc move provides a sliver of hope  that there is an alternative venue for  justice  especially they say when their own  justice system in the country  has failed them jamella allendogen al  jazeera  manila
 

